# Resource Tracks

Characters have three aspects: **Mind**, **Body**, and **Soul**. Each aspect has a
Resilience trait and two resource tracks: one slow-recovering track and one
fast-recovering track.

| Aspect | Resilience | Slow track | Fast track |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Mind | **Will** | **Plasticity** | **Attention** |
| Body | **Health** | **Injury** | **Fatigue** |
| Soul | **Conviction** | **Spirit** | **Mana** |

The Resilience trait for an aspect determines the number of slots at **each
severity** on both of that aspect's tracks.

## Severity Grades

Resource marks have four grades:

| Grade | Penalty |
| --- | ---: |
| Minor | 0 |
| Middling | -1 |
| Major | -2 |
| Final | -4 |

**Injury** calls its Final grade **Fatal**.

For each track, apply only the penalty from its highest occupied grade. Multiple
marks at the same grade do not increase that track's penalty. Penalties from
different tracks stack.

For example, a character with a Final-or-Fatal mark on all six tracks suffers
-24 to rolls.

## Applying Marks

When an Effect produces a magnitude against a personal resource, compare that
magnitude to the appropriate Resilience trait, R:

- magnitude 0: no mark;
- positive magnitude less than R: one Minor mark;
- R to less than 2 x R: one Middling mark;
- 2 x R to less than 3 x R: one Major mark;
- 3 x R to less than 4 x R: one Final mark;
- each additional full multiple of R adds another Final mark.

A single sufficiently large incident can therefore inflict several Final marks at
once.

For example, against Health 4, an Injury Effect of 1-3 causes a Minor Injury,
4-7 a Middling Injury, 8-11 a Major Injury, 12-15 one Fatal Injury, 16-19 two
Fatal Injuries, and 20-23 three Fatal Injuries. An Effect of 24 or more fills all
four Fatal slots from an otherwise uninjured state and kills the character.

## Promotion

If every slot at a grade is already filled and another mark of that grade is
applied, clear all marks at that grade and add one mark at the next higher grade.
Promotion can cascade.

Final is the top grade and does not promote further. When all Final slots on a
track are filled, that track is **exhausted**.

## Recovery

A recovery step always works on the lowest occupied grade.

- If Minor marks are present, clear one Minor mark.
- Otherwise, remove one Middling mark and fill all Minor slots.
- Otherwise, remove one Major mark and fill all Middling slots.
- Otherwise, remove one Final mark and fill all Major slots.

Fast tracks take one recovery step **per action**. Slow tracks take one recovery
step **per day**.

## Exhaustion

Exhausting a fast track renders the character unconscious. It may also spill Minor
harm into the corresponding slow track; the exact spillover rule is still under
development.

Exhausting **Injury** kills the character. The catastrophic consequences of
exhausting Plasticity or Spirit remain to be defined.

## Ordinary Uses

Some ordinary actions interact with these tracks directly:

- mundane weapons normally attack **Injury**;
- mundane exertion costs **Fatigue**;
- study costs **Attention**;
- supernatural Domains define their own resource costs and interactions.

## Costs

A resource **Cost** uses the same Effect resolution as any other Power.

A Cost has a rating and a target resource. Roll the Cost rating; there is no
Application roll. Nothing reduces the Cost rating before it is rolled: armor,
external defenses, and other mitigation do not discount it. The user's Resilience
does not reduce the roll; it determines the severity of the marks produced by the
rolled Effect magnitude.

Apply those marks to the target resource normally.

A Cost roll can produce zero magnitude, in which case it inflicts no mark.

Tier applies to Cost rolls in the same way it applies to other Effect rolls. The
exact Tier mechanics are still under development.
